 PostgreSQL server can handle multiple concurrent connections from clients.\",\"locator\":\"Architectural Fundamentals, connection handling\",\"origin\":\"PostgreSQL official documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\",\"responseId\":\"mtqvh49r38e0zl\"}],\"method\":\"Applies to PostgreSQL's documented client/server architecture.\",\"comparability\":\"This establishes networked client access, not a throughput claim or a configuration-independent limit on connections.\",\"confidence\":\"High for the architectural facts because exact current official passages were retrieved.\",\"disposition\":\"retain\"}],\"gaps\":[\"Conflicting-writer behavior for both systems was not extracted as an exact primary passage within the five-call allowance. The highest-impact follow-up is to retrieve SQLite WAL write-transaction documentation and PostgreSQL current transaction-isolation/explicit-locking passages covering same-row conflicts, aborts, and retry requirements.\",\"Online-backup semantics are not an owned slot in this assignment; no backup conclusion is supplied here.\",\"Verify the deployed SQLite and PostgreSQL versions and the actual SQLite journal mode, filesystem topology, PostgreSQL isolation level, and connection configuration before applying these documented behaviors.\"],\"stopReason\":\"All three required retrieval actions were discovered and successfully invoked; the five-invocation retrieval allowance is exhausted. Exact primary support was obtained for the owned reader/writer-overlap and deployment facts, while unsupported conflicting-writer details are explicitly limited.\"}","usage":{"input":23315,"output":4566,"cacheRead":49152,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0.1112524},"exitCode":0,"stderr":"","finishedAt":1788763360025,"value":{"assignment":"concurrency","discovered":["extensions.web_search","extensions.fetch_content","extensions.get_search_content"],"calls":[{"ref":"extensions.web_search","args":{"queries":["site:sqlite.org wal.html SQLite WAL concurrent writers readers same host network filesystem","site:postgresql.org/docs current MVCC concurrent updates writers transaction isolation","site:postgresql.org/docs current backup pg_basebackup online backup concurrent connections"],"numResults":5,"workflow":"none"},"retrieved":true,"detail":"3/3 queries successful; 15 total results.","responseId":"mtqvgvehtf27bc"},{"ref":"extensions.fetch_content","args":{"urls":["https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","https://www.sqlite.org/useovernet.html","https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html","https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html","https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html"],"mode":"readable"},"retrieved":true,"detail":"5/5 URLs fetched; 70,385 total extracted characters.","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"},{"ref":"extensions.get_search_content","args":{"responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl","urlIndex":0,"findText":["readers do not block writers","only one writer at a time","same host computer"]},"retrieved":true,"detail":"3 matching passages returned; no match for \"only one writer at a time\".","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"},{"ref":"extensions.get_search_content","args":{"responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl","urlIndex":2,"offset":0,"limit":5000},"retrieved":true,"detail":"Complete PostgreSQL MVCC introduction returned, 1,612 characters.","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"},{"ref":"extensions.get_search_content","args":{"responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl","urlIndex":4,"offset":0,"limit":5000},"retrieved":true,"detail":"Complete PostgreSQL architecture section returned, 1,844 characters.","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"rows":[{"id":"sqlite-reader-writer-overlap","slot":"concurrency","finding":"SQLite WAL documents concurrent reading and writing: readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"WAL provides more concurrency as readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers. Reading and writing can proceed concurrently.","locator":"Advantages, introductory WAL section","origin":"SQLite official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"counterevidence":"The same retrieved page says this is \"mostly true\" and that some WAL-mode queries can return SQLITE_BUSY; applications should be prepared for that circumstance.","method":"Applies when the database is operating in SQLite WAL mode. This is documented locking behavior, not a throughput measurement.","comparability":"Directly comparable only to reader/writer overlap. It does not establish behavior for overlapping conflicting write transactions.","confidence":"High for the stated reader/writer behavior because the exact current official WAL passage was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},{"id":"postgres-reader-writer-overlap","slot":"concurrency","finding":"PostgreSQL documents that MVCC read locks do not conflict with write locks, so reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html","title":"13.1. Introduction","passage":"The main advantage of using the MVCC model of concurrency control rather than locking is that in MVCC locks acquired for querying (reading) data do not conflict with locks acquired for writing data, and so reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading.","locator":"MVCC introduction, paragraph 2","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"method":"Applies to PostgreSQL's documented MVCC behavior. This is not a measurement and does not define outcomes when writers contend for the same row.","comparability":"Comparable to SQLite only for reader/writer overlap; write/write conflict handling was not extracted as an exact passage in this stream.","confidence":"High for the stated reader/writer behavior because the exact current official MVCC passage was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},{"id":"sqlite-wal-deployment-boundary","slot":"deployment","finding":"SQLite WAL requires every process using the database to be on the same host; it does not work over a network filesystem.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem. This is because WAL requires all processes to share a small amount of memory and processes on separate host machines obviously cannot share memory with each other.","locator":"Disadvantages, introductory WAL section","origin":"SQLite official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"method":"Applies to SQLite in WAL mode where processes access the database directly.","comparability":"This is a file-access deployment constraint, unlike PostgreSQL's client/server network interface.","confidence":"High because the exact current official WAL limitation was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},{"id":"postgres-client-server-deployment","slot":"deployment","finding":"PostgreSQL documents a client/server model: server and client may be on different hosts over TCP/IP, and the server can handle multiple concurrent client connections.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html","title":"1.2. Architectural Fundamentals","passage":"As is typical of client/server applications, the client and the server can be on different hosts. In that case they communicate over a TCP/IP network connection.","locator":"Architectural Fundamentals, client/server description","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html","title":"1.2. Architectural Fundamentals","passage":